@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction
@cindex introduction

@code{tempo} is a library for date and time manipulation, not unlike
local-time. It attempts to bring a more ergonomic and modern experience
with conditions, restarts, and predictable naming.

@menu
* Scope::
* Installing and loading::
* Five-minute tour::
@end menu

@node Scope
@section Scope
@cindex scope

The core distinction in @code{tempo} is between @dfn{calendar values}
and @dfn{elapsed time}:

@table @asis
@item @dfn{Calendar values} (@code{date}, @code{wall-time}, @code{datetime})
Positions on a calendar or clock. These values are @dfn{naive}: they carry
no timezone information.
@item @dfn{Elapsed time} (@code{duration})
A displacement between two points in time. @code{tempo} models elapsed
time with two independent slots: integer calendar months and exact rational
seconds.
@end table

The types in @code{tempo} are structure values with read-only slots; the
public API never mutates them. They compare with @code{equalp} and work as
@code{:test 'equalp} hash-table keys.

@code{tempo} deliberately never guesses a timezone, a month length, or a
rounding policy. When an operation requires information the value cannot
supply, it signals a condition or offers a restart. Domain errors are reported
through specific subtypes of @code{tempo-error}, while type mismatches
signal direct @code{type-error}.

Zoned instants (@code{timestamp}) and ranges (@code{interval}) are planned
for later phases (@pxref{Limits and roadmap}).

@node Installing and loading
@section Installing and loading
@cindex installation
@cindex ASDF

@code{tempo} is not yet on quicklisp, you can install it locally by
cloning the repo to your @code{local-projects} directory, which is
@code{~/quicklisp/local-projects/} by default.

@example
git clone https://github.com/raynei86/tempo.git ~/quicklisp/local-projects
@end example

Or alternatively via @url{https://roswell.github.io/, roswell}:
@example
ros install raynei86/tempo
@end example

Then it can be loaded through @code{ASDF}:
@lisp
(asdf:load-system "tempo")
@end lisp

@node Five-minute tour
@section Five-minute tour
@cindex tour

@lisp
(tempo:date 2026 10 30)
@result{} #<DATE 2026-10-30>

(tempo:wall-time 9 30)
@result{} #<WALL-TIME 09:30:00>

(tempo:datetime 2026 10 30 9 30)
@result{} #<DATETIME 2026-10-30T09:30:00>

(tempo:seconds 90)
@result{} #<DURATION PT1M30S>

(tempo:datetime-difference
 (tempo:datetime 2026 10 31 9 30)
 (tempo:datetime 2026 10 30 9 30))
@result{} #<DURATION P1D>
@end lisp

The printed forms are for humans. They assume that @code{tempo} is
accessible in the current package (for example, because your package
uses it); if it is not, your Lisp may qualify the structure type with
its home package. For storage and interchange, use the ISO formatters
described in @ref{Formatting}.

@node Choosing a type
@chapter Choosing a type
@cindex choosing a type

The first decision is whether you are storing a calendar position or a
displacement. The second is whether a timezone is part of the value.
If two types seem to fit, prefer the smaller one: a birthday is a
@code{date}, not a midnight @code{datetime}; opening hours are a
@code{wall-time}, not a @code{datetime} on an arbitrary day. Using the
smaller type prevents accidental timezone and daylight-saving
assumptions.

@menu
* Type chooser::
* Capability map::
@end menu

@node Type chooser
@section Type chooser

@multitable @columnfractions .30 .18 .52
