@node Exactness
@section Exactness
@cindex exactness
@cindex rationals

@code{tempo} stores time as exact rationals, not as floating-point
numbers.
The unit constructors accept any real number at the boundary and
calls @code{rationalize} on it:

@lisp
(tempo:duration-seconds (tempo:seconds 0.1))
@result{} 1/10
(tempo:duration-seconds (tempo:seconds (rational 0.1)))
@result{} 13421773/134217728
@end lisp

@code{rationalize} approximates a float into a rational representation
within its rounding precision; thus @code{0.1} becomes @code{1/10}. If
you need the exact mathematical value of a float, use @code{rational}
instead.

@node Months are not seconds
@section Months are not seconds
@cindex months
@cindex inexact durations

A @code{duration} has two independent slots: integer calendar months
and exact seconds. The slots are not interchangeable, because a month
has no fixed length. Asking for seconds when the value has months
signals @code{inexact-duration-error}:

@lisp
(tempo:in-seconds (tempo:months 1))
@result{} error: INEXACT-DURATION-ERROR
@end lisp

The same rule applies in the other direction: @code{in-months} and
@code{in-years} require the seconds slot to be zero. A year is twelve
months, so @code{(tempo:years 1)} and @code{(tempo:months 12)} are the
same duration:

@lisp
(tempo:duration= (tempo:years 1) (tempo:months 12))
@result{} T
@end lisp

But a month is not thirty days. @code{duration=} compares the slots
literally, while ordering needs a common unit:

@lisp
(tempo:duration= (tempo:months 1) (tempo:days 30))
@result{} NIL
@end lisp

@code{(tempo:duration< (tempo:months 1) (tempo:days 30))} signals
@code{inexact-duration-error}, because months and seconds cannot be
ordered without a reference date. Use @code{duration=} when you mean
``the same two slots'', and use calendar arithmetic when you mean
``the same amount of time from this date''.

@node Storage and display
@section Storage and display
@cindex normalization
@cindex destructuring

Storage keeps the two slots separate. Time units are converted to
seconds within the seconds slot, while calendar months stay in the
months slot; display decomposes both for readability. A duration of
thirteen months prints as one year and one month, but the stored months
slot is still thirteen:

@lisp
(tempo:duration :months 13)
@result{} #<DURATION P1Y1M>

(tempo:duration-months (tempo:duration :months 13))
@result{} 13
@end lisp

Likewise, ninety minutes are stored as 5400 seconds and printed with
hours and minutes:

@lisp
(tempo:duration :minutes 90)
@result{} #<DURATION PT1H30M>

(tempo:duration-seconds (tempo:duration :minutes 90))
@result{} 5400
@end lisp

@code{destructure-duration} truncates toward zero and returns the
components in ISO order:

@lisp
(tempo:destructure-duration (tempo:duration :months 13 :days 1))
@result{} 1, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0
@end lisp

Do not rely on the printed form to recover the slots. Read
@code{duration-months} and @code{duration-seconds}, or use
@code{destructure-duration} when you want a human decomposition.

@node Signs
@section Signs
@cindex signs
@cindex negative durations

A negative duration is written with a leading minus sign:

@lisp
(tempo:format-duration-iso (tempo:duration :seconds -10))
@result{} "-PT10S"
@end lisp

A duration may have a positive months slot and a negative seconds slot,
or the reverse. Such a value has no overall sign, so
@code{duration-plusp}, @code{duration-minusp}, and @code{duration-abs}
signal @code{inexact-duration-error}. Avoid constructing mixed-sign
durations; if you need a signed displacement, keep the same sign on both
slots.

@node Calendar conventions
@section Calendar conventions
@cindex calendar
@cindex weekday
@cindex ISO week

@code{tempo} uses the
@url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proleptic_Gregorian_calendar,
proleptic Gregorian calendar}. Year 0 exists and is a leap year, and
the constructors accept any integer year, including negative years:

@lisp
(tempo:valid-date-p 0 2 29)
@result{} T

(tempo:leap-year-p 0)
@result{} T

(tempo:date -221 1 1)
@result{} #<TEMPO.DATE:DATE -221-01-01>
@end lisp

ISO 8601 formatting is only defined for years 0000 through 9999; see
@ref{Formatting types}.

Weekdays are numbered 1 through 7, with Monday as 1 and Sunday as 7.
Weeks run Monday through Sunday:

@lisp
(tempo:day-of-week (tempo:date 2026 10 30))
@result{} 5

(tempo:start-of-week (tempo:date 2026 10 30))
@result{} #<DATE 2026-10-26>

(tempo:end-of-week (tempo:date 2026 10 30))
@result{} #<DATE 2026-11-01>
@end lisp

@code{next-weekday} and @code{previous-weekday} are strict: if the date
already falls on the requested weekday, they move to the next or
previous week. This is usually what calendar code wants, and it makes
the ``nth weekday'' recipe simple.

ISO week numbers are not the same as calendar year and day-of-year.
@code{iso-calendar} returns three values, and the ISO year can differ
from the calendar year at the boundary:

@lisp
(multiple-value-list (tempo:iso-calendar (tempo:date 2021 1 1)))
@result{} (2020 53 5)
@end lisp

January 1, 2021 is a Friday in ISO week 53 of 2020.

@code{rata-die} numbers days so that 0001-01-01 is day 1 and
0000-12-31 is day 0. Day numbers are the exact, timezone-free way to
add days and measure spans:

@lisp
(tempo:rata-die (tempo:date 0 12 31))
@result{} 0
@end lisp

@node Naive time
@section Naive time
@cindex naive time
@cindex timezones

A @code{datetime} has no timezone information. Two datetimes with the
same fields are @code{equalp} even if one is interpreted in Tokyo and
the other in New York, and comparing them says nothing about which
instant is earlier.

@code{today}, @code{yesterday}, and @code{tomorrow} read the process's
local timezone through @code{get-decoded-time}. They are convenience
functions, not a clock protocol; a test that depends on them is
timezone-dependent and can fail around midnight.

@lisp
(tempo:today)
@result{} #<DATE ...>
@end lisp

If you need an exact instant, wait for @code{timestamp}, or convert
explicitly and record the zone alongside the value. Do not order
datetimes from different zones.

@node Equality 
@section Equality
@cindex equality
@cindex hash tables
@cindex FASL

The values in @code{tempo} are structures with read-only slots. @code{equalp}
compares their contents; @code{equal} compares identity, so it is false
for two separately constructed values:

@lisp
(equalp (tempo:date 2026 10 30) (tempo:date 2026 10 30))
@result{} T

(equal (tempo:date 2026 10 30) (tempo:date 2026 10 30))
@result{} NIL
@end lisp

Use @code{equalp} for hash tables:

@lisp
(let ((table (make-hash-table :test 'equalp)))
  (setf (gethash (tempo:date 2026 10 30) table) :friday)
  (gethash (tempo:date 2026 10 30) table))
@result{} :FRIDAY
@end lisp

@node Formatting types
@section Formatting types
@cindex formatting
@cindex round trip

Every type has an ISO 8601 formatter: @code{format-date-iso},
@code{format-wall-time-iso}, @code{format-datetime-iso}, and
@code{format-duration-iso}. Each also has a stream variant named
@code{write-TYPE-to-iso}, and a @code{cl:format} directive:

@lisp
(format nil "at ~/tempo:format-datetime/"
        (tempo:datetime 2026 10 30 9 30))
@result{} "at 2026-10-30T09:30:00"
@end lisp

The printed forms are:

@table @code
@item #<DURATION PT1H30M>
@itemx #<DATE 2026-10-30>
@itemx #<WALL-TIME 09:30:00.5>
@itemx #<DATETIME 2026-10-30T09:30:00.5>
@code{print-object} for the corresponding values.
@end table

ISO formatting is not lossless serializer for every
possible value. Two caveats exist:

@itemize
@item
Fractional seconds are printed through a double float. A
non-terminating rational such as @code{1/3} becomes
@code{"PT0.3333333333333333S"}. The value is still exact; only the
string is approximate.
@item
Date formatting is defined for years 0000 through 9999. The
constructors accept any integer year, but years outside that range do
not have a conforming ISO string.
@end itemize

For round-tripping your own data, store the ISO string and parse it at
the boundary. @code{tempo} does not provide a parser yet
(@pxref{Limits and roadmap}).

