@node Validating input
@section Validating input
@cindex validation

Use the total predicates at the boundary. They return @code{nil} for
nonsense instead of signalling:

@lisp
(defun safe-date (year month day)
  (when (tempo:valid-date-p year month day)
    (tempo:date year month day)))

(safe-date 2026 2 30)
@result{} NIL

(safe-date 2026 2 28)
@result{} #<DATE 2026-02-28>
@end lisp

When you need to explain why the input was rejected, handle the
condition and read its slots:

@lisp
(handler-case (tempo:date 2026 2 30)
  (tempo:invalid-date-error (c)
    (list :invalid
          (tempo:invalid-date-month c)
          (tempo:invalid-date-day c)
          (tempo:invalid-date-reason c))))
@result{} (:INVALID 2 30 "February 2026 has 28 days, not 30 days")
@end lisp

The same pattern works for @code{valid-wall-time-p} and
@code{valid-datetime-p}. Remember that a non-integer field signals a
plain @code{type-error}, not a @code{tempo-error}
(@pxref{Errors and restarts}).

Once data is inside your program, stop validating and let the
constructors enforce the invariant. Do not pass unchecked user input
to @code{date} or @code{wall-time} and then interpret the resulting
condition as a validation result.

@node Ages and birthdays
@section Ages and birthdays
@cindex age
@cindex birthday
@cindex February 29

An age is a calendar calculation, not a division by 365. Compare the
month and day in the target year, and decide what February 29 means.
The @code{min} below clamps February 29 to February 28 in non-leap
years:

@lisp
(defun birthday-in-year (birthday year)
  (let ((month (tempo:date-month birthday)))
    (tempo:date year
                month
                (min (tempo:date-day birthday)
                     (tempo:days-in-month year month)))))

(defun age-on (birthday on)
  (let ((this-year (birthday-in-year birthday (tempo:date-year on))))
    (if (tempo:date<= this-year on)
        (- (tempo:date-year on) (tempo:date-year birthday))
        (1- (- (tempo:date-year on) (tempo:date-year birthday))))))

(defun next-birthday (birthday today)
  (let ((this-year (birthday-in-year birthday (tempo:date-year today))))
    (if (tempo:date< this-year today)
        (birthday-in-year birthday (1+ (tempo:date-year today)))
        this-year)))
@end lisp

@lisp
(age-on (tempo:date 2000 2 29) (tempo:date 2026 2 28))
@result{} 26

(next-birthday (tempo:date 2000 2 29) (tempo:date 2026 3 1))
@result{} #<DATE 2027-02-28>
@end lisp

This @code{next-birthday} includes today when today is the birthday.
If you need strictly later, compare with @code{tempo:date<} and roll to
the next year when equal.

If your policy is ``March 1 in non-leap years'' instead, compare the
month and day explicitly rather than clamping. The important point is
that the policy is in your code, where it can be reviewed.

@node Month-end dates
@section Month-end dates
@cindex month end
@cindex billing

To clamp a day to the end of a month, use @code{replace-date} and let
the restart rewrite the overflow:

@lisp
(defun billing-date (year month day)
  (handler-bind ((tempo:invalid-date-error
                   (lambda (c)
                     (declare (ignore c))
                     (invoke-restart 'tempo:clamp-to-end-of-month))))
    (tempo:replace-date (tempo:date year month 1) :day day)))

(billing-date 2026 2 31)
@result{} #<DATE 2026-02-28>
@end lisp

The restart is offered by @code{replace-date} and
@code{replace-datetime}; see @ref{Month-end overflow} for the
semantics.

When you only need the last day, @code{end-of-month} is simpler:

@lisp
(tempo:end-of-month (tempo:date 2024 2 10))
@result{} #<DATE 2024-02-29>
@end lisp

@xref{Month-end overflow} for the condition and restart details.

@node Nth and last weekday
@section Nth and last weekday
@cindex nth weekday
@cindex last weekday

@code{next-weekday} is strict, so start one day before the month and
step forward. Starting one day after the month makes the backwards
version just as short:

@lisp
(defun nth-weekday (year month weekday n)
  (loop with d = (tempo:date-from-rata-die
                  (1- (tempo:rata-die (tempo:date year month 1))))
        repeat n
        do (setf d (tempo:next-weekday d weekday))
        finally (return d)))

(defun last-weekday (year month weekday)
  (let ((end (tempo:end-of-month (tempo:date year month 1))))
    (tempo:previous-weekday
     (tempo:date-from-rata-die (1+ (tempo:rata-die end)))
     weekday)))
@end lisp

@lisp
(nth-weekday 2026 11 :thursday 3)
@result{} #<DATE 2026-11-19>

(last-weekday 2026 11 :friday)
@result{} #<DATE 2026-11-27>
@end lisp

@var{n} must be at least 1. A large @var{n} can spill into the next
month; if that matters, check that the result still has the requested
month before using it.

@node Adding days and spans
@section Adding days and spans
@cindex adding days
@cindex day difference

Day numbers make day arithmetic exact and timezone-free:

@lisp
(defun date+days (date n)
  (tempo:date-from-rata-die (+ (tempo:rata-die date) n)))

(defun days-between (a b)
  (- (tempo:rata-die a) (tempo:rata-die b)))
@end lisp

@lisp
(date+days (tempo:date 2026 12 31) 1)
@result{} #<DATE 2027-01-01>

(days-between (tempo:date 2026 3 1) (tempo:date 2026 2 28))
@result{} 1
@end lisp

For second-level arithmetic on a datetime, use @code{datetime+} and
@code{datetime-}; for the exact span between two datetimes, use
@code{datetime-difference}. Adding calendar months to a date is a
different operation and is not implemented yet
(@pxref{Limits and roadmap}).

@node Opening hours
@section Opening hours
@cindex opening hours
@cindex overnight span

Wall times compare by seconds since midnight. A half-open interval
@code{[opening, closing)} handles the usual case, and the comparison
wraps when closing is earlier than opening:

@lisp
(defun open-p (opening closing now)
  (if (tempo:wall-time<= opening closing)
      (and (tempo:wall-time<= opening now)
           (tempo:wall-time< now closing))
      (or (tempo:wall-time<= opening now)
          (tempo:wall-time< now closing))))
@end lisp

@lisp
(open-p (tempo:wall-time 9 0) (tempo:wall-time 17 0) (tempo:wall-time 12 0))
@result{} T

(open-p (tempo:wall-time 22 0) (tempo:wall-time 2 0) (tempo:wall-time 1 30))
@result{} T

(open-p (tempo:wall-time 22 0) (tempo:wall-time 2 0) (tempo:wall-time 2 0))
@result{} NIL
@end lisp

This is wall-clock logic. It does not know about daylight-saving
transitions, holidays, or the date. If those matter, you need a
timezone-aware layer, which @code{tempo} does not have yet.

@node Elapsed time
@section Elapsed time
@cindex elapsed time
@cindex benchmarking

For elapsed time inside one process, use the implementation's internal
clock and convert the difference to an exact duration:

@lisp
(let ((start (get-internal-real-time)))
  ;; ... work ...
  (tempo:seconds
   (/ (- (get-internal-real-time) start)
      internal-time-units-per-second)))
@end lisp

The division is exact if the units divide evenly; otherwise the result
is an exact rational. @code{get-internal-real-time} measures wall-clock
time and is suitable for timeouts and benchmarks.
@code{get-internal-run-time} measures processor time instead.

This is not a source of calendar timestamps. There is no clock
protocol yet, so tests that need a fixed ``now'' must pass the date in.

@node Universal time and Unix epoch
@section Universal time and Unix epoch
@cindex universal time
@cindex Unix time
@cindex epoch

Universal time is a non-negative integer count of seconds since
1900-01-01 GMT. The timezone argument to @code{decode-universal-time}
and @code{encode-universal-time} is in hours west of GMT; pass 0 for
UTC. These helpers project a universal time to a naive datetime and
back:

@lisp
(defun datetime-from-universal-time (ut &optional (zone 0))
  (multiple-value-bind (second minute hour day month year)
      (decode-universal-time ut zone)
    (tempo:datetime year month day hour minute second)))

(defun universal-time-from-datetime (dt &optional (zone 0))
  (multiple-value-bind (year month day hour minute second)
      (tempo:destructure-datetime dt)
    (encode-universal-time second minute hour day month year zone)))
@end lisp

@lisp
(datetime-from-universal-time 0)
@result{} #<DATETIME 1900-01-01T00:00:00>

(universal-time-from-datetime (tempo:datetime 1970 1 1))
@result{} 2208988800
@end lisp

Unix time counts seconds from 1970-01-01 UTC. Computing it from day
numbers avoids universal time's 1900 epoch and works for earlier dates:

@lisp
(defun unix-time (dt)
  (+ (* 86400 (- (tempo:rata-die (tempo:datetime-date dt))
                 (tempo:rata-die (tempo:date 1970 1 1))))
     (* 3600 (tempo:datetime-hour dt))
     (* 60 (tempo:datetime-minute dt))
     (tempo:datetime-second dt)))

(defun datetime-from-unix-time (seconds)
  (multiple-value-bind (days rem) (floor seconds 86400)
    (multiple-value-bind (hour rem) (truncate rem 3600)
      (multiple-value-bind (minute second) (truncate rem 60)
        (tempo:datetime-from-parts
         (tempo:date-from-rata-die
          (+ (tempo:rata-die (tempo:date 1970 1 1)) days))
         (tempo:make-wall-time
          :hour hour :minute minute :second second))))))
@end lisp

@lisp
(unix-time (tempo:datetime 1970 1 1))
@result{} 0

(datetime-from-unix-time 0)
@result{} #<DATETIME 1970-01-01T00:00:00>
@end lisp

Both helpers treat the datetime as UTC. If the value really represents
a local time, the conversion is a projection and the zone must be
recorded separately. Neither helper accounts for leap seconds; POSIX
time and the @code{tempo} wall clock both ignore them.

Because universal time is non-negative, dates before 1900 cannot be
represented at all. For earlier dates, use the Unix helper or work
directly with @code{rata-die}.

@node Sorting and hash keys
@section Sorting and hash keys
@cindex sorting
@cindex hash tables

The comparison functions are ordinary two-argument predicates, so they
work with @code{sort} and @code{remove-duplicates}:

@lisp
(sort (list (tempo:date 2026 1 2) (tempo:date 2025 12 31))
      #'tempo:date<)
@result{} (#<DATE 2025-12-31> #<DATE 2026-01-02>)
@end lisp

@code{sort} is destructive; pass a copy if the original list matters.
For deduplication, use the type's equality predicate:

@lisp
(remove-duplicates
 (list (tempo:date 2026 10 30) (tempo:date 2026 10 30))
 :test #'tempo:date=)
@result{} (#<DATE 2026-10-30>)
@end lisp

For hash tables, use @code{equalp}:

@lisp
(let ((table (make-hash-table :test 'equalp)))
  (setf (gethash (tempo:date 2026 10 30) table) :friday)
  (gethash (tempo:date 2026 10 30) table))
@result{} :FRIDAY
@end lisp

Do not use @code{eq} or @code{equal} for value lookup unless you
deliberately want identity.

@node ISO week reporting
@section ISO week reporting
@cindex ISO week
@cindex week number

@code{iso-calendar} returns the ISO year, week, and weekday. The ISO
year can differ from the calendar year at the boundary:

@lisp
(multiple-value-bind (year week day)
    (tempo:iso-calendar (tempo:date 2021 1 1))
  (format nil "~4,'0d-W~2,'0d-~d" year week day))
@result{} "2020-W53-5"
@end lisp

The weekday value is 1 through 7 with Monday as 1, matching
@code{day-of-week}. For display, keep the ISO year, not the calendar
year; otherwise the week number and the year can disagree.

@node FASL constants
@section FASL constants
@cindex FASL
@cindex compile-file

The structures in @code{tempo} define @code{make-load-form}, so they can appear in
compiled code and be dumped into FASL files:

@lisp
(defconst *epoch* (tempo:date 1970 1 1))
@end lisp

@node Errors and restarts
@chapter Errors and restarts
@cindex conditions
@cindex restarts
@cindex errors

@code{tempo} separates two tiers of mistakes. A value that is the wrong type
signals a plain @code{type-error}. A value of the right type that does
not name a real date or time signals a subtype of @code{tempo-error},
which carries the offending fields and a human-readable reason.

@menu
* What signals what::
* Handling errors:
* Month-end overflow::
@end menu

@node What signals what
@section What signals what

@table @asis
@item @code{date}, @code{make-date}
@code{invalid-date-error} for an impossible date, @code{type-error}
for a non-integer.
@item @code{wall-time}, @code{make-wall-time}
@code{invalid-time-error} for an impossible time, @code{type-error}
for a non-integer.
@item @code{datetime}, @code{make-datetime}
It's composed of a @code{date} and @code{wall-time}, so those same
errors can bubble up.
@item @code{replace-date}, @code{replace-datetime}
@code{invalid-date-error}; the @code{clamp-to-end-of-month} restart is
offered when the day overflows.
@item @code{in-seconds}, @code{in-days}, and friends
@code{inexact-duration-error}; keep the months and seconds separate.
@item @code{duration-floor}, @code{duration-ceiling}, @code{duration-round}
@code{inexact-duration-error} when the duration has months; snap the
seconds slot first.
@item @code{duration-plusp}, @code{duration-minusp}, @code{duration-abs}
@code{inexact-duration-error} for mixed signs; avoid mixed-sign
durations.
@item @code{duration<}, @code{duration<=}, @code{duration>}, @code{duration>=}
@code{inexact-duration-error} for incomparable units; compare in one
unit, or use @code{duration=}.
@item @code{duration/}, @code{duration-divide}
@code{inexact-duration-error} or @code{division-by-zero}; check the
divisor and keep months whole.
@item @code{duration} with a non-integer @code{:months}
@code{type-error}; use an integer, or use @code{:years}.
@end table

@node Handling errors
@section Handling errors

@code{handler-case} unwinds the stack and returns a value, so it can be
used for reporting or replacing a failed operation:

@lisp
(handler-case (tempo:date 2026 2 30)
  (tempo:invalid-date-error (c)
    (list (tempo:invalid-date-month c)
          (tempo:invalid-date-day c)
          (tempo:invalid-date-reason c))))
@result{} (2 30 "February 2026 has 28 days, not 30 days")
@end lisp

@code{handler-bind} runs the handler in the dynamic extent where the
condition was signalled, so it can invoke a restart and let the original
operation finish with a different policy:

@lisp
(handler-bind ((tempo:invalid-date-error
                 (lambda (c)
                   (declare (ignore c))
                   (invoke-restart 'tempo:clamp-to-end-of-month))))
  (tempo:replace-date (tempo:date 2024 1 31) :month 2))
@result{} #<DATE 2024-02-29>
@end lisp

@node Month-end overflow
@section Month-end overflow
@cindex month end
@cindex clamp-to-end-of-month

@code{replace-date} and @code{replace-datetime} validate the resulting
date. When the requested day overflows the target month, they signal
@code{invalid-date-error} and offer the @code{clamp-to-end-of-month}
restart; the example under @ref{Handling errors} shows
the invocation.

@node Limits and roadmap
@chapter Limits and roadmap
@cindex limits
@cindex roadmap

@c TODO(user): This chapter should be in your voice. Keep it honest:
@c users need to know what is missing before they design around it.

@menu
* Current limits::
* Roadmap::
@end menu

@node Current limits
@section Current limits
@cindex missing features

@xref{Capability map} for the quick answer on individual operations.
The deferred work falls into two groups: calendar arithmetic (Phase 3)
and zoned time (Phase 4). Everything else the capability map marks
``No'' is deliberate scope for the current release, not a bug. In
particular, there is no ISO parser, no default timezone or test clock,
no non-Gregorian calendar, and no leap-second handling; the seconds
field is always less than 60.

The known rough edges are documented where they arise:
@ref{Formatting types} for ISO output, @ref{Signs} for
mixed-sign durations, and @ref{Naive time} for @code{today}.

@node Roadmap
@section Roadmap
@cindex phase 3
@cindex phase 4

This is a general roadmap of the project:

@table @asis
@item Phase 1 (complete)
Package layout, conditions, and exact durations.
@item Phase 2 (complete)
Naive calendar values: @code{date}, @code{wall-time}, and
@code{datetime}, with comparisons, seconds arithmetic, and formatting.
@item Phase 3
Month-bearing arithmetic, @code{date+} and @code{date-}, calendar
differences, and the month-end restarts.
@item Phase 4
@code{timestamp}, @code{timezone}, @code{interval}, a clock protocol,
and daylight-saving resolution.
@end table

Until then, the workarounds in @ref{Common tasks} cover day arithmetic,
validation, weekdays, and interoperability. If a missing feature is
blocking you, that is useful information for the roadmap.

@node Duration
@chapter Duration
@cindex duration
@cindex ISO 8601
@cindex arithmetic

@deftp {Data Type} duration
An exact span of time with two slots: an integer number of calendar
@var{months} (a year is 12 months) and a rational number of
@var{seconds}. The time-based unit constructors accept any real
number and rationalize it, so there is no floating point drift; the
calendar constructors are stricter (see @ref{Duration Units}).
@end deftp

@lisp
(tempo:duration :hours 3/2)
@result{} #<DURATION PT1H30M>
(tempo:milliseconds 1)
@result{} #<DURATION PT0.001S>
@end lisp

The default print representation uses ISO 8601. A duration with a
months component cannot be converted to a fixed number of seconds and
signals @code{inexact-duration-error} where exactness is required.

@menu
* Constructors and accessors: Duration Constructors.
* Unit constructors: Duration Units.
* Conversions: Duration Conversions.
* Arithmetic: Duration Arithmetic.
* Predicates and comparison: Duration Predicates.
* Formatting: Duration Formatting.
@end menu

@node Duration Constructors
@section Constructors and accessors

@deffn {Function} make-duration &key months seconds
The strict raw constructor. @var{months} must be an integer and
@var{seconds} a rational; both default to 0. Prefer the unit
constructors for everyday use.
@end deffn

@deffn {Function} duration-p object
Return true when @var{object} is a duration.
@end deffn

@deffn {Function} duration-months duration
@deffnx {Function} duration-seconds duration
Return the integer months or the exact rational seconds slot.
@end deffn

@deffn {Function} duration &key years months weeks days hours minutes seconds milliseconds microseconds nanoseconds
Return a duration combining any of the supplied units.
